{"step_index":233,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-23T13:41:36Z","content":"That is a great suggestion. Navigating large interactive charts on a mobile phone (especially the narrow screens of iPhones) can definitely be frustrating when the chart steals your scrolling or the margins waste too much space.\n\nI've just pushed a **Mobile UI Optimization** update specifically for this! Here is what I changed for the charts:\n\n1. **Tightened Margins:** I removed the wasted white space around the edges so the chart stretches fully edge-to-edge on your iPhone screen.\n2. **Horizontal Legend:** I moved the legend (which explains what the lines mean) from the side to the top. This prevents it from squishing the candlestick chart horizontally on narrow screens.\n3. **Disabled the Mode Bar:** I hid those tiny, hard-to-tap Plotly tools (lasso, zoom, box select) that clutter the top right corner. \n4. **Improved Touch Scrolling:** I switched the drag mode to `pan` by default and tweaked the scroll settings so you can easily swipe past the chart without getting \"stuck\" inside it!\n\nGive your Streamlit Cloud app a refresh on your iPhone 16 Pro and let me know how the charts feel now!","thinking":"**Prioritizing Tool Specificity**\n\nI'm focusing intently on tool specificity. I've been refining my approach to avoid unnecessary use of bash commands for file creation or appending, opting for dedicated tools like `>` and `>>` respectively. I am also working to use `grep_search` more for searching, rather than general grep commands. I must remember to use the more efficient tools.\n\n\n"}
